


IDENTD(8)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		IDENTD(8)



NNAAMMEE
     identd, in.identd - TCP/IP IDENT protocol server

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     //uussrr//lliibbeexxeecc//[[iinn..]]iiddeennttdd [--ii|--ww|--bb [--tt<<sseeccoonnddss>>] [--uu<<uuiidd>>]
     [--gg<<ggiidd>>] [--pp<<ppoorrtt>>] [--aa<<aaddddrreessss>>] [--cc<<cchhaarrsseett>>]
     [--CC[<<kkeeyyffiillee>>]] [--oo] [--ee] [--ll] [--VV] [--mm] [--NN] [--dd]
     [--FF<<ffoorrmmaatt>>] [kkeerrnneellffiillee[kkmmeemmffiillee]]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     iiddeennttdd is a server which implements the TCP/IP proposed
     standard IDENT user identification protocol as specified in
     the RFC 1413 document.

     iiddeennttdd operates by looking up specific TCP/IP connections
     and returning the user name of the process owning the con-
     nection.  It can optionally return other information instead
     of a user name.

AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
     The --ii flag, which is the default mode, should be used when
     starting the daemon from iinneettdd with the "nowait" option in
     the //eettcc//iinneettdd..ccoonnff file. Use of this mode will make iinneettdd
     start one iiddeennttdd daemon for each connection request.

     The --ww flag should be used when starting the daemon from
     iinneettdd with the "wait" option in the //eettcc//iinneettdd..ccoonnff file .
     This is the prefered mode of operation since that will start
     a copy of iiddeennttdd at the first connection request and then
     iiddeennttdd will handle subsequent requests without having to do
     the nlist lookup in the kernel file for every request as in
     the --ii mode above. The iiddeennttdd daemon will run either for-
     ever, until a bug makes it crash or a timeout, as specified
     by the --tt flag, occurs.

     The --bb flag can be used to make the daemon run in standalone
     mode without the assistance from iinneettdd..  This mode is the
     least prefered mode since a bug or any other fatal condition
     in the server will make it terminate and it will then have
     to be restarted manually. Other than that it has the same
     advantage as the --ww mode in that it parses the nlist only
     once.

     The --tt<<sseeccoonnddss>> option is used to specify the timeout limit.
     This is the number of seconds a server started with the --ww
     flag will wait for new connections before terminating. The
     server is automatically restarted by iinneettdd whenever a new
     connection is requested if it has terminated. A suitable
     value for this is 120 (2 minutes), if used. It defaults to
     no timeout (i.e. will wait forever, or until a fatal condi-
     tion occurs in the server).




